import
	../../html

intro-java html[]()
	- section "Calling Java functions",
		- p quote
			Consider a typical Java "Hello, world" program:
		- code-block quote
			import java.lang.System;

			class Main \{
				public static void main(String[] args) \{
					System.out.println("Hello, world!");
				\}
			\}

		- p "To translate it directly to Keen:"
		- keen-not-runnable quote
			import
				java/io/PrintStream
				java/lang/System

			main void() java, global
				trusted println out, "Hello, world!"

		- aside-small "These examples can't run in the browser, so you'll have to test them on the command line."

		- p quote
			An import beginning with {keyword "java/"} imports a class from the Java standard library.
			{br}
			(Replace {keyword "."} with {keyword "/"}.
			So {code "java/io/PrintStream"} imports {code "java.io.PrintStream"} .)
		- p quote
			Keen is not Java and doesn't have many of Java's features, such as classes and methods.
			{br}
			When Keen imports a Java class,
			the class' public properties and methods are translated to functions.
		- bullets
			- quote
				A {static} field becomes a function with no arguments. The class name is omitted.
				{br}
				For example, Java's {code "System.out"} becomes {fn-out} in Keen.
			- quote
				A {static} method can be called directly. The class name is omitted.
				{br}
				For example, Java's {code "Math.sqrt"} becomes {function "sqrt"} in Keen.
			- quote
				An instance field becomes a function taking the class as an argument.
				{br}
				For example, Java's {code "Point.x"} instance field becomes a function {fn-x} in Keen.
			- quote
				For an instance method, a {this} argument of the class' type is prepended to the arguments.
				{br}
				For example, the instance method {function "println"} takes a {code "java/io/PrintStream"}
				(such as {fn-out}) as its first argument.
			- quote
				A class constructor becomes a function named {fn-new}, and a function named the same as the class.
				(Use whichever you prefer.)

		- p "All functions that come from Java have the {spec-java}, {global_}, and {unsafe} specs."

		- p quote
			In Java, methods on a class can be called anywhere without importing the class.
			Keen doesn't work like that; if you want to call a method, you need to import the class.
			{br}
			The Java program above only needed {code "import java.lang.System;"},
			while the Keen version needs to import both {code "java/io/PrintStream"} and {code "java/lang/System"}.

	- section "Null values",
		- p quote
			In Java, all reference types have a {null} value.
			{br}
			Keen considers {null} values to be {unsafe},
			so don't don't mark a call to a function that comes from Java as {trusted_}
			unless you are sure it won't return {null}.

		- p quote
			{fn-java-null} is an {unsafe} function returning a Java null value for a type.
			{br}
			As a convenience, {std-function "keen/java", "is-null"}{sp}{var-a} is equivalent to
			{fn-reference-equal}{sp}{var-a}{comma}{fn-java-null}.
		- p "The below example reads a directory, handling the case where it does not exist."
		- keen-not-runnable quote
			import
				keen/java
				java/lang/String
				java/io/File

			main void() java, global
				log get-files "some-dir"

			get-files string[](dir-name string) java, global
				trusted
					dir File = dir-name.to::String,
					files String buffer = list dir
					forbid is-null files else error "Directory does not exist"
					for file in files
						to::string file

	- section "Casting",
		- p quote
			Java subclasses implicitly convert to their superclasses (and interfaces).
		- p quote
			For reference types, {fn-reinterpret-cast} corresponds to a Java cast.
			{br}
			This can be used to convert a Java class to a subclass.
		- keen-not-runnable quote
			import
				keen/unsafe
				java/lang/Object
				java/lang/String

			main void() java, global, unsafe
				a String = "hello"
				b Object = a
				c String = reinterpret-cast b
				log c.to

	- section "Generics",
		- p "Keen supports Java generic types such as {code "List"}."
		- keen-not-runnable quote
			import
				java/lang/Iterable
				java/lang/String
				java/util/Iterator
				java/util/Arrays
				java/util/List
				keen/java

			main void() java, global, unsafe
				buf String buffer = "hello", "world"
				list String List = asList buf
				iterable String Iterable = list
				iterator String Iterator = iterator iterable
				log to::string next iterator
				log to::string next iterator
				log hasNext iterator

	- section "Type translation",
		- p "Java types translate to the following Keen types:"
		- table
			- thead
				- th "Java"
				- th "Keen"
			- tbody
				- tr
					- td "{code "void"}"
					- td "{void}"
				- tr
					- td "{code "bool"}"
					- td "{bool}"
				- tr
					- td "{code "byte"}"
					- td "{int8}"
				- tr
					- td "{code "short"}"
					- td "{int16}"
				- tr
					- td "{code "int"}"
					- td "{int32}"
				- tr
					- td "{code "long"}"
					- td "{int}"
				- tr
					- td "{code "float"}"
					- td "{float32}"
				- tr
					- td "{code "double"}"
					- td "{float64}"
				- tr
					- td "{code "t[]"}"
					- td "{t}{sp}{buffer}"

		- p quote
			Since Java {Array}s are mutable, they are translated as {buffer} instead of {array}.
			{br}
			If a Java method treats an {Array} argument as read-only.
			you could use {std-function "keen/col/buffer", "cast-mutable"} to pass in a Keen {array}.
			{br}
			If a Java method returns an {Array} that will never be mutated,
			you could use {std-function "keen/col/buffer", "cast-immutable"} to get it as a Keen {array}.

		- keen-not-runnable quote
			import
				java/io/PrintStream
				java/lang/String
				java/lang/System

			main void() java, global, unsafe
				bytes int8[] = 104, 101, 108, 108, 111
				# cast-mutable because 'new String()' does not mutate its argument
				s String = bytes.cast-mutable,
				println out, s
		- p quote
			There are additional functions in {std-module "keen/unsafe"} for converting between types
			that have the same runtime representation, such as {type "char8 buffer"} and {type "nat8[]"}.
			(These work in any target, not just Java.)

		- aside "Strings",
			- p quote
				Keen's {string} and {code "java/lang/String"} are two different types.
				{br}
				There are {std-function "keen/java", "to"} functions for converting.
				{br}
				A string literal can directly create a {code "java/lang/String"}.

	- section "Implementing Java interfaces",
		- p quote
			Keen record types can implement Java interfaces directly.
			It works just like implementing a Keen interface.
		- keen-not-runnable quote
			import
				java/lang/Object
				java/util/Arrays
				java/util/Comparator

			main void() global, java, unsafe
				a point buffer =
					- 1, 2
					- 2, 0
					- 1, 1
				# sort is from 'java/lang/Arrays'
				sort a, point-comparator
				log a

			point record
				x nat
				y nat

			point-comparator record() point Comparator case
			equals bool(_ point-comparator, _ Object)
				# Comparator requires this, but it won't actually be used
				throw unreachable
			compare int32(_ point-comparator, a point, b point)
				match a <=> b
				as less then -1
				as equal then 0
				as greater then 1

		- aside-small quote
			This is just an example; in a real program you would use the standard
			{std-function "keen/col/sort", "sort"}.

		- p quote
			Keen doesn't make use of Java generics, so:
		- bullets
			- "Java's {function "sort"} function acts on an {type "Object buffer"} ."
			- "The interface is just {type "Comparator"} with no type parameter."
			- "The comparator accepts {type "Object"} parameters and downcasts them using {fn-reinterpret-cast}."

		- aside "Extending Java classes",
			- p quote
				Keen does not have a way to subclass a Java class.
				{br}
				If you need to use a Java API that requires you to subclass its class,
				write the subclass in Java.
			- p quote
				Maybe define an interface yourself and have the subclass delegate to it,
				allowing you to write the actual implementation in Keen by implementing that interface.

	- section "Nested classes",
		- p quote
			A nested class like {code "Map.Entry"} is imported as {namespace "Map$Entry"}.
			The name of the type is just {type "Entry"}.
		- keen-not-runnable quote
			import
				java/util/Iterator
				java/util/Map
				java/util/Map$Entry
				java/util/Set

			main void() global, java, unsafe
				map (nat, string) Map = ofEntries
					- entry 1, "one"
					- entry 2, "two"
				iterator (nat, string) Entry Iterator = iterator entrySet map
				entry = next iterator
				log entry.getKey, entry.getValue


	- section "Keen types",
		- p quote
			There are no guarantees about how Keen translates to Java.
			{br}
			A Keen {interface_} isn't always a Java {interface_}.
			{br}
			A Keen {record_} isn't always a Java {record_}.
			{br}
			So don't expect to use Java reflection.

	- section "JARs",
		- p quote
			To use Java classes from outside the standard library, they must be in a JAR file.
			{br}
			The {code "imports"} section of {code "config.kid"} supports JAR files.
			(See {intro-link configs}.)
			{br}
			For example:
		- keen-not-runnable "config.kid", quote
			imports:
				"org/foo": "./libs/foo.jar"
		- p quote
			With the above configuration,
			all imports starting with {code "org/foo"} will resolve to classes from {code "libs/foo.jar"}.
		- keen-not-runnable quote
			import
				# Gets 'org/foo/Foo.class' from 'libs/foo.jar'
				org/foo/Foo

			main void() global, java, unsafe
				someStaticMethod

		- aside-small "Keen doesn't use Java's {code "CLASSPATH"} for anything."
		- p quote
			When you build the program, all classes (even ones not directly referenced)
			from configured JAR files will be copied to the output, so it builds to a standalone executable JAR.

-Array html-node()
	code "Array"
-fn-java-null html-node()
	std-function "keen/java", "java-null"
-fn-out html-node()
	function "out"
-this html-node()
	code "this"
-static html-node()
	code "static"
